You have promised to send the titles of your reading group's text
notes. It seems economical to keep \texttt{*.txt} in a variable and
paste it into a command; after all, typing that pattern directly
worked. But there is a difference between supplying the pattern,
supplying the literal characters \texttt{*.txt}, and asking the shell
to print each matching name. The difference lies in what the shell
does between reading the command and running it.

\begin{objectives}
\item Predict the usual expansion stages for an ordinary POSIX shell argument.
\item Tell the difference between a home-directory shortcut, a variable lookup and captured output.
\item Preserve an argument boundary or deliberately match filenames without guessing.
\end{objectives}

\section{Change one layer at a time}
\label{sec:26-order}
\index{expansion}
\index{field splitting}
\index{pathname expansion}

The examples here use a fresh \texttt{sh} session. Type each setup
line individually and stop if the first reports an error or the
second does not print the new directory. \texttt{mktemp -d} is a
common GNU/BSD convenience, not a POSIX requirement; if unavailable,
use Chapter~\ref{ch:12}'s procedure for making a new, empty folder
and record its full path in \texttt{practice}. Each redirection
creates one fixture; use these only in that new folder.

\begin{terminal}
practice=$(mktemp -d)
cd "$practice" && pwd
printf '%s\n' 'Friday selection' > 'Long title.txt'
printf '%s\n' 'Tuesday selection' > Short.txt
\end{terminal}

In Chapter~\ref{ch:25}, quotes protected spaces and brackets. Now
consider the other side of that protection: the shell normally
transforms some words before handing them to the command. The
general name for these transformations is \emph{expansion}. For
ordinary arguments in \texttt{sh}, the main stages are shown in
Figure~\ref{fig:26-order}. Some special contexts (including
assignments and redirection operands) have their own rules.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\input{figures/chapter-26/order.tikz}
\caption{The usual expansion stages for ordinary POSIX shell words.}
\label{fig:26-order}
\VisualDescription{Value-producing expansions come first. Field splitting may produce multiple words. Pathname expansion can then match filenames. The shell removes the syntax quotes last.}
\end{figure}

The quotes still guide earlier stages even though quote removal is
listed last. Think of the shell marking which parts are protected
while reading the line. It does not temporarily forget the quotes
and match all the stars, then take the quote marks off at the end
and hope for the best. Conversely, a quote character \emph{from a
value} was not used to read the command line. It is not a new
quoting instruction.

\begin{terminal}
pattern='*.txt'
printf '<%s>\n' "$pattern"
printf '<%s>\n' $pattern
printf '<%s>\n' ./*.txt
\end{terminal}

The first command prints \texttt{<*.txt>} once. The unquoted
expansion in the second command leaves the star eligible for
pathname expansion and prints two names. The third prints the
same names with \texttt{./} prefixed, providing a useful clue
that each is an actual relative pathname. The order of the two
names can depend on the locale, which is the operating
environment's set of rules governing things like sorting and
character interpretation. To check correctness here, look at
membership and boundaries rather than order.

Notice the long title appears as one line between one set of
brackets in the last two runs. Splitting does not start over
after the shell has produced matching names. Put another way,
splitting answers whether a \emph{value} supplies multiple fields;
pathname expansion answers which existing names match a
\emph{pattern}. They are separate decisions. This makes the
direct \cmd|./*.txt| preferable to an unquoted stored pattern
when you can write the pattern directly.

\begin{quicktable}{p{1.25in}YY}
\toprule
Source in the command & Main action & Typical result \\
\midrule
Bare tilde at the start & Substitute a home directory & A home path, not a search through the working directory \\
\texttt{\$\{name\}} & Substitute a stored value & One protected argument if surrounded by double quotes \\
\texttt{\$(command)} & Capture standard output & Text with final newlines stripped \\
Unquoted glob & Match names after splitting & Zero or more matches, or a shell-specific no-match result \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

\section{The home shortcut has a place}
\label{sec:26-tilde}
\index{tilde expansion}

Try this locally without posting the output: the first two
arguments will usually print the same home directory, while the
last prints a literal tilde. The unquoted tilde is written at the
start of a word, where the shell recognizes it as a shortcut.
Quotes before the tilde defeat that recognition.

\begin{terminal}
printf '<%s>\n' ~ "$HOME" "~"
printf '<%s>\n' ~/'Reading group'
\end{terminal}

The second command appends a protected space-containing suffix.
It need not name a real folder because \texttt{printf} only
prints the argument. \cmd|"$HOME/Reading group"| is an everyday
alternative that keeps the entire argument quoted. Sometimes a
tool's configuration file accepts a tilde in paths too; that is
a feature of that tool, not a guarantee the shell makes on its
behalf. And writing \cmd|'~/Reading group'| does not ask the
shell to expand the tilde later. It passes a relative string
beginning with \texttt{\textasciitilde}.

\section{Capture text, not a set of names}
\label{sec:26-capture}
\index{command substitution}

You used \cmd|$(mktemp -d)| to get a new directory path instead
of manually copying it from the terminal. The shell runs the
command inside \cmd|$(...)| and substitutes its standard output,
dropping all trailing newline characters. It does not capture
standard error unless you arrange a redirection. Compare these
two displays:

\begin{terminal}
printf '<%s>\n' "$(printf 'queue\n\n')"
printf '<%s>\n' "$(printf 'first\nsecond\n')"
\end{terminal}

The first prints one line, \texttt{<queue>}. The second prints
two visible lines across one argument: the opening bracket and
\texttt{first} are on line one; \texttt{second} and the closing
bracket are on line two. The internal newline survives. If the
output must preserve the exact byte stream, including its
trailing newlines, save it to a new file instead of a variable.
Shell variables are not arbitrary binary containers either.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\input{figures/chapter-26/notes.tikz}
\caption{A quoted capture preserves internal newlines, not trailing ones.}
\label{fig:26-notes}
\VisualDescription{A command emits first, a newline, second and a final newline. Capture removes the final newline. Double quotes preserve the internal newline in one argument.}
\end{figure}

As Figure~\ref{fig:26-notes} suggests, capture can produce one
piece of text containing multiple line breaks. Double quotes
will not reconstruct separate filenames from that text. In the
other direction, unquoting a capture to get separate words
does not reconstruct a file list either: it splits at whitespace
under the default settings, and star or question-mark characters
in those words may be matched as patterns. A direct glob gives
each resulting filename its own argument. That is why a glob is
the right tool for gathering names in the current directory,
while a quoted capture is right for collecting, say, one line
of text from a fixture.

\begin{terminal}
title=$(cat 'Long title.txt')
printf '<%s>\n' "$title"
printf '<%s>\n' $title
\end{terminal}

The first display is \texttt{<Friday selection>}. The second
has one bracketed word per line. The same file was opened
both times; the second display causes a split only when
\texttt{title} is read back as an unquoted argument. A shell
assignment by itself, such as \cmd|title=$(cat ...)|, is not an
ordinary argument and does not split the result this way.
Keeping the display quoted is sensible even if the current
value has no spaces: the habit is about the boundary, not
one day's input.

\section{Make a prediction before expanding}
\label{sec:26-predict}

The default separators for field splitting are space, tab
and newline. This is not the same as the blanks separating
words while the shell initially reads your line. A value may
contain a space that never appeared in the command source;
that space can still split an unquoted expansion. \texttt{IFS}
is the shell parameter that controls splitting. Leave it alone
in this chapter: changing it globally would make ordinary
examples harder to predict for the sake of a special case.

\begin{terminal}
label="'two words'"
printf '<%s>\n' $label
printf '<%s>\n' "$label"
\end{terminal}

The first run shows \texttt{<'two>} and \texttt{<words'>};
the stored apostrophes did not protect the blank. The second
prints the whole value, including the two apostrophes. The
value passed through the shell is not fresh shell source:
the apostrophes were generated too late to be recognized as
quoting syntax. Instead of forcing a second interpretation,
choose the intended boundaries when building the first command.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\input{figures/chapter-26/glob.tikz}
\caption{The glob creates argument slots after splitting has finished.}
\label{fig:26-glob}
\VisualDescription{One unquoted pattern leads to two matched pathnames, each in a separate slot. Long title.txt retains its space inside one slot.}
\end{figure}

\begin{dialoguebox}{A list that is not a list}
\Brian{The directory listing gives me one title per line. Can't I capture it and use it as a list of names?}
\Stephen{For the two simple names you first tried, it might seem to work.}
\Brian{One of mine is Long title. Doesn't putting each name on its own line solve that?}
\Stephen{No. Ordinary field splitting can split at the space too. And the newline itself could occur in a filename.}
\Brian{Then quote the output? At least no name gets split.}
\Stephen{True, but now all the names are in one argument. A direct glob gives you separate arguments without squeezing them into text first.}
\DialogueConclusion{A text stream is not an argument list.}
\end{dialoguebox}

\begin{quicktable}{YY}
\toprule
What you intend & What to write in this fixture \\
\midrule
One filename with a known spelling & \texttt{'./Long title.txt'} \\
One stored name whose value may change & \texttt{"\$name"} after assigning the name \\
All visible text filenames as separate arguments & \texttt{./*.txt}; check what happens when no names match \\
The characters of a filename pattern for a utility that interprets it & \texttt{'*.txt'}, for example as an operand to \texttt{find -name} \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

An unmatched POSIX shell pattern normally remains unchanged.
Try this harmless probe for a deliberately absent suffix:

\begin{terminal}
printf '<%s>\n' ./*.bak
\end{terminal}

The result \texttt{<./*.bak>} is not evidence that a backup
exists. By contrast, the ordinary native Zsh default is an
error on this unmatched pattern. A typical interactive Bash
normally keeps the word as written, but its settings can
change that behavior. Run these chapter examples under
\texttt{sh} and see Chapter~\ref{ch:29} before moving a script
to a different language.

\section{Assemble a reliable preview}
\label{sec:26-preview}

The reading group wants every visible text note whose name
starts with a known prefix. Use a sample prefix, not a new
pattern to be interpreted from a message. First print the
prefix literally; then join that protected prefix to an
unquoted star. The prefix could later contain brackets or a
space; only the star outside the quotes should be active.

\begin{terminal}
prefix='Long '
printf 'Prefix: <%s>\n' "$prefix"
printf 'Candidate: <%s>\n' ./"$prefix"*.txt
\end{terminal}

You should see the blank before the first closing bracket,
then a result line for \texttt{./Long title.txt}. A blank
is part of this prefix. Had you copied it into another line
outside quotes, it would have ended the shell word instead.
If the candidate looks wrong, inspect the first output
before editing the second command. It is easier to discover
that the prefix is empty by looking for \texttt{<>} than by
guessing from a surprisingly long list of matched filenames.

Change the prefix to \texttt{Early } and repeat the second
line. In the unmodified fixture the shell leaves the unmatched
pattern word intact. A candidate line is consequently not
the same as confirmation that a file exists. Run this probe
as a separate experiment in the named setup directory:

\begin{terminal}
prefix='Early '
printf 'Candidate: <%s>\n' ./"$prefix"*.txt
test -f './Early *.txt'
printf 'Is that exact name a file? %s\n' "$?"
\end{terminal}

The candidate text contains the original star and the test
reports one. The test is quoted to ask about that exact
name. Don't detect an unmatched glob by searching the result
for a star: the characters legal in a filename include a
literal star. A future script can test the candidate's type
directly instead. A preview that mistakes a placeholder for a
real file is inconvenient; feeding that same result into a
write operation would be a worse mistake.

When you troubleshoot a complicated expression, keep the
order shown earlier in view and change only one element at
a time. Read the unexpanded source first: what was inside
quotes before any substitution? Look at the variable
value second: is it the single intended word, possibly
with embedded spaces? Third, check whether any unquoted
part is allowed to split or match. Finally, distinguish
what the command receives from what it later prints.
You can replace the command with the bracket probe for
that final inspection. None of these steps requires changing
the filenames to avoid spaces or switching your default
interactive shell. The expression should state which pieces
are data and which characters are instructions.

One other convenience differs among shells: in Bash and
native Zsh, brace expansion can generate literal alternatives
before pathname matching, as in \texttt{\{first,second\}.txt}.
That spelling is not the mechanism this book uses to produce
a portable two-argument list. Write \texttt{first.txt
second.txt} when you mean two fixed names, regardless of
whether either exists. Do not confuse generating strings
with browsing what is on disk; a literal alternative can be
produced even when nothing with that name was ever created.

\section{Exercises and cleanup}

\begin{exercise}{Let the shell match only the suffix}
\label{ex:26-suffix}
Assign the word \texttt{Long title} to \texttt{stem}, then
print the result of matching that exact stem followed by a
wildcard suffix. Leave the star active without exposing the
blank inside the stem.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
stem='Long title'
printf '<%s>\n' ./"$stem".*
unset stem
\end{terminal}
Expect \texttt{<./Long title.txt>}. The quote marks protect the
expanded stem but not the star outside the pair. \texttt{unset}
removes the practice variable, not the file.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Predict the number of arguments}
\label{ex:26-lines}
Capture the two lines \texttt{one} and \texttt{two}. Predict
the visible brackets if the expansion is quoted and if it is
unquoted. Do not change \texttt{IFS}.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
value=$(printf 'one\ntwo\n')
printf '<%s>\n' "$value"
printf '<%s>\n' $value
unset value
\end{terminal}
The quoted form prints one pair of brackets across two lines.
The unquoted form prints two pairs, one on each line. There
are no extra files to remove.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Confirm what a failed match means}
\label{ex:26-nomatch}
In the disposable practice directory, determine whether the
unchanged \texttt{./*.bak} text names a regular file. The
option \texttt{-f} of \texttt{test} checks for a regular file.
Display its exit status immediately with \cmd|$?|: zero
means yes, and one means no.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
test -f './*.bak'
printf 'Status: %s\n' "$?"
\end{terminal}
Expect \texttt{Status: 1}. Quoting is correct because this
check asks about the literal name reported by the probe, not
whether some other name matches. No files are created.
\end{solution}

Confirm the working directory with \texttt{pwd}. Remove only
the two known fixtures; \texttt{rm -i} asks before removing
each one. If you made additional fixtures, inspect their
names rather than switching to a wildcard for cleanup.

\begin{terminal}
pwd
rm -i './Long title.txt' './Short.txt'
cd / && rmdir "$practice"
exit
\end{terminal}

\begin{takeaways}
\item For ordinary POSIX shell arguments, splitting precedes pathname expansion.
\item Command substitution captures text and drops trailing newlines.
\item Quote expansions that should supply one argument; leave only deliberate glob characters active.
\end{takeaways}

